# ============================================================
# Mapping: BFO ↔ IES Ontology
#
# Finding from structural matching: 
# IES does not use any class expressions, so there were no structural matches. 
# A structural match was produced by enriching IES with a class restriction.
# The 19 resulting candidates (State paired with BFO classes having universal restrictions) were rejected: none is a superclass or equivalent of a 4D state. The mappings below target bfo:occurrent directly
# ============================================================

@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix a:    <http://ies.data.gov.uk/ontology/ies4#> .
@prefix b:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/> .

# A fundamental mismatch between BFO and IES is that BFO treats temporal and spaciotemporal regions as distinct from the continuants and occurrents that may happen within them.
# IES treats space and time and the processes and things it contains as a single unit. 
# This produced a mapping of both ies:State and ies:Element as subclasses bfo:Occurrent - this is broad enough to permit the relationship without contradicting IES' core premises.

a:State rdfs:subClassOf b:BFO_0000003 .

a:Element rdfs:subClassOf b:BFO_0000003 .